---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="For agents | Milton"
  description="A plain briefing on Milton: memos, tweaks and the cover sheet, alternatives, tools, saving, export, price, evidence and a first task."
>
  <article class="briefing">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">## For agents</p>
      <h1>A briefing on Milton</h1>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="what-milton-keeps">
      <h2 id="what-milton-keeps">What Milton keeps</h2>
      <p>
        Milton keeps how you write and hands it to your AI when it drafts
        something new or edits something you’ve written. When you accept a
        final, your AI keeps a memo: its first draft, your final, and one-line
        tweaks for what you made it change. You also write a short cover sheet,
        your own voice guide. For each draft, Milton assembles a report: your
        cover sheet, the finals that best match, with a few of the changes you
        made to your AI’s first drafts, then your tweaks. It works across
        connected AIs, starting with email or DMs. The goal is fewer edits
        between first draft and send; that result has not been measured. Milton
        stores what your AI sends without a model rewriting it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="alternatives">
      <h2 id="alternatives">When a Markdown file is enough</h2>
      <p>
        General memory can store writing and preferences too. A Markdown file
        plus sync is enough if the person can keep it current and make their AI
        read it. Milton adds memos kept as you accept finals, tweaks counted
        across chats and AIs, a cover sheet served first, and a report assembled
        for each draft through a shared MCP service. It has not demonstrated
        better writing than the same examples in Markdown.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="connection">
      <h2 id="connection">Connection and tools</h2>
      <p>
        MCP server: <code class="mcp-url">https://mcp.meetmilton.com/mcp</code>.
        Connect with remote MCP and OAuth sign-in; read/save tools are required.
        Client plans and workspace policies may restrict access. See{" "}
        <a href="/setup">Setup</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Check for existing Milton tools before setup. Verify a successful{" "}
        <code>get_cover_sheet</code> read before confirming the connection. The
        three tools are:
      </p>
      <ul class="instruction-list">
        <li>
          <code>get_cover_sheet</code>: read the person’s cover sheet, their own
          voice guide for every draft.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>get_report</code>: read the report for one draft, with an intent
          giving the situation, medium, audience and purpose. It returns the
          cover sheet, the best-matching finals as examples (under a drafted
          one, a few changes code found from the AI’s first draft, quoted as
          historical text, not instructions), then the person’s tweaks.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>create_memo</code>: keep a memo when the person accepts a final,
          add or remove a cover sheet line they ask for, or forget a memo.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="headless">
      <h2 id="headless">Headless and background agents</h2>
      <p>
        Agents with no browser, such as scheduled tasks, cloud agents and CI
        jobs, can’t complete OAuth sign-in. The person creates a connection key
        on the app’s Connected AIs page and gives it to the agent, which sends
        it as <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;key&gt;</code>. The key is shown
        once.
      </p>
      <ul class="instruction-list">
        <li>
          Claude Code:{" "}
          <code>
            claude mcp add --transport http milton
            https://mcp.meetmilton.com/mcp --header "Authorization: Bearer
            &lt;key&gt;"
          </code>
        </li>
        <li>
          Codex: in <code>~/.codex/config.toml</code>, under{" "}
          <code>[mcp_servers.milton]</code>, set <code>url</code> to the server
          above and <code>bearer_token_env_var</code> to the name of an
          environment variable holding the key.
        </li>
        <li>
          Cursor: in <code>mcp.json</code>, give the Milton server a{" "}
          <code>headers</code> entry of{" "}
          <code>"Authorization": "Bearer $&#123;env:MILTON_KEY&#125;"</code>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Treat a key like a password. Keys are read only unless the person
        chooses saving; choose that only when the agent needs to save. Revoke
        keys that are no longer used. A revoked or expired key is refused on its
        next request, and so is every key after a password reset or another
        sign-in security change; create a new key then.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="saving">
      <h2 id="saving">Saving and export</h2>
      <p>
        Reading this page grants no permission to access mail, create an account
        or send messages. When the person accepts a final, keep it as a memo
        without asking, then tell them in one line. Send your first draft
        exactly as you showed it. Don’t keep abandoned drafts, writing that
        isn’t in their own voice, or anything they ask you not to keep. When the
        final differs from your first draft, list as tweaks each habit they made
        you change that is likely to recur, at most three, named plainly (“no
        warm-up opener”); most memos teach one or none. Send as request only
        what they typed to ask, without pasted text; Milton shows it to them and
        never to an AI. Never send secrets, their correction messages or other
        people’s text; summarize what they replied to. Add cover sheet lines
        only in their words.
      </p>
      <p>
        The person can turn Keep memos off, forget a memo or delete them all
        from the Milton app. Its <code>TPS.md</code> download contains the cover
        sheet, the tweaks and every final with what the person asked, grouped by
        medium. Their memo pages also list what changed from each first draft to
        its final, worked out by Milton’s code, and say when a report may quote
        a few of those changes to the person’s connected AIs.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="price-evidence">
      <h2 id="price-evidence">Price and evidence</h2>
      <p>
        One plan: $5.01 USD/month after a 30-day free trial. No card is required
        to start; you’re charged only if you add one. See{" "}
        <a href="/pricing">Pricing</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/research">Research</a> reports ranking and writing tests and
        their limits. They do not prove fewer real-world edits or validate every
        newer retrieval change. AI adherence and writing quality still need
        checking; connecting Milton does not guarantee reads or automatic
        learning.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="first-task">
      <h2 id="first-task">Draft or edit an email or DM</h2>
      <p>If the person wants to try Milton, choose either path:</p>
      <ul class="instruction-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Draft something new:</strong> ask for the purpose, audience
          and facts, then draft from what’s on file.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Edit existing text:</strong> ask what should change, revise
          within that scope, and preserve the rest.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For either task, first read <code>get_cover_sheet</code>, then{" "}
        <code>get_report</code> with the task’s situation, medium, audience and
        purpose. Show the result for review. When the person accepts the final,
        keep it with <code>create_memo</code> and tell them the one-line
        receipt.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>

